      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 0.18,
          "end": 2.25,
          "en": "-: Let's talk about wrapper classes.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 2.25,
          "end": 4.35,
          "en": "Now we have talked about primitive variables, right?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 4.35,
          "end": 5.46,
          "en": "Example, if you want to create,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 5.46,
          "end": 7.11,
          "en": "if you want to store a number like five,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 7.11,
          "end": 9.39,
          "en": "of course you will store that in an INT variable",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 9.39,
          "end": 10.44,
          "en": "or maybe short, maybe byte,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 10.44,
          "end": 11.79,
          "en": "that's your choice.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 11.79,
          "end": 15.48,
          "en": "If you want to work with a value which is point values,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 15.48,
          "end": 17.61,
          "en": "let's say 6.5 or something like that,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 17.61,
          "end": 19.41,
          "en": "you will be using double or float.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 19.41,
          "end": 21.39,
          "en": "And then if you want to store a character,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 21.39,
          "end": 23.28,
          "en": "you will use a char, right?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 23.28,
          "end": 25.71,
          "en": "So Java does provide you primitive data types,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 25.71,
          "end": 28.1,
          "en": "and that's what makes Java",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 28.1,
          "end": 31.95,
          "en": "99.99% object-oriented.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 31.95,
          "end": 34.5,
          "en": "That's right, Java is not purely object-oriented",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 34.5,
          "end": 36,
          "en": "because say when you talk about a language",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 36,
          "end": 37.98,
          "en": "like object-oriented programming language,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 37.98,
          "end": 40.05,
          "en": "it means that everything should be object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 40.05,
          "end": 41.25,
          "en": "But that's not the case with Java.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 41.25,
          "end": 42.99,
          "en": "Java says, okay, we are still going to work",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 42.99,
          "end": 45.96,
          "en": "with something which is not extending object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 45.96,
          "end": 49.2,
          "en": "As we know, every class in Java extends object class,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 49.2,
          "end": 50.46,
          "en": "but not the primitive values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 50.46,
          "end": 51.99,
          "en": "If I talk about INT, float,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 51.99,
          "end": 53.79,
          "en": "they are primitive types.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 53.79,
          "end": 55.683,
          "en": "They are not object types, right?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 56.55,
          "end": 60.15,
          "en": "It's good because it helps Java to improve the performance",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 60.15,
          "end": 62.91,
          "en": "because it directly works with the fixed site value, right?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 62.91,
          "end": 66.22,
          "en": "So it is not saved in a heap memory as an object,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 66.22,
          "end": 68.43,
          "en": "which will be time consuming.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 68.43,
          "end": 70.71000000000001,
          "en": "So what it says is, if you want to show the value,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 70.71000000000001,
          "end": 72.53999999999999,
          "en": "you can do that directly in primitive,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 72.53999999999999,
          "end": 74.1,
          "en": "but then there are certain features,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 74.1,
          "end": 75.36,
          "en": "there are certain frameworks in Java,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 75.36,
          "end": 77.01,
          "en": "which only works with objects.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 77.01,
          "end": 78.87,
          "en": "Example, let's say in future,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 78.87,
          "end": 80.52,
          "en": "we are gonna talk about collection framework.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 80.52,
          "end": 83.13,
          "en": "In collection, if you want to store values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 83.13,
          "end": 84.78,
          "en": "So just to give you a heads up,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 84.78,
          "end": 88.17,
          "en": "collection is basically similar to array at this point.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 88.17,
          "end": 90.48,
          "en": "Just remember that. And we can have multiple values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 90.48,
          "end": 93.6,
          "en": "In array, we can have these primitive types,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 93.6,
          "end": 94.62,
          "en": "but not in collection.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 94.62,
          "end": 96.42,
          "en": "You need to have object type,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 96.42,
          "end": 98.64,
          "en": "and that's why Java says, let me introduce something.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 98.64,
          "end": 102.09,
          "en": "So in Java, we got this concept called a wrapper class.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 102.09,
          "end": 103.95,
          "en": "Now what's a wrapper class?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 103.95,
          "end": 106.17,
          "en": "For every primitive type,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 106.17,
          "end": 109.44,
          "en": "we are going to have a class for it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 109.44,
          "end": 111.09,
          "en": "Okay? So if you want to work with INT,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 111.09,
          "end": 113.31,
          "en": "you can work with integer, which is a class.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 113.31,
          "end": 116.78999999999999,
          "en": "And this integer class basically extends the object class.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 116.78999999999999,
          "end": 119.31,
          "en": "You have a char, we have character as a class,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 119.31,
          "end": 121.35,
          "en": "we have double, we have a double as a class,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 121.35,
          "end": 123.48,
          "en": "and this goes on, maybe it is short.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 123.48,
          "end": 124.71,
          "en": "Then we have byte.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 124.71,
          "end": 128.85,
          "en": "For every primitive type, we have a class for it, okay?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 128.85,
          "end": 132.899,
          "en": "And it's quite good if you want to work with a framework,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 132.899,
          "end": 135,
          "en": "which only supports objects.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 135,
          "end": 136.95,
          "en": "But yes, if you want to improve the performance",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 136.95,
          "end": 139.74,
          "en": "and there's no restrictions, primitive still makes sense.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 139.74,
          "end": 141.06,
          "en": "And that's why most of the examples",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 141.06,
          "end": 142.74,
          "en": "we have used primitive type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 142.74,
          "end": 144.15,
          "en": "But again, there are certain requirements",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 144.15,
          "end": 146.4,
          "en": "where you have to use classes.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 146.4,
          "end": 148.59,
          "en": "Now how do we do that? So let's cut back here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 148.59,
          "end": 149.82,
          "en": "Let's say I have a INT variable",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 149.82,
          "end": 152.37,
          "en": "INT NUM is equal to seven.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 152.37,
          "end": 155.28,
          "en": "Now how do I store this value into a class type",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 155.28,
          "end": 156.54,
          "en": "or the object type?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 156.54,
          "end": 159.21,
          "en": "So the class we have is called integer, right?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 159.21,
          "end": 161.37,
          "en": "So let me get a variable called NUM1.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 161.37,
          "end": 164.16,
          "en": "So that's my reference object.",
